    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/Text4ShellPov.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant a correct fix must enforce: StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator() / StringLookupFactory.INSTANCE.interpolatorStringLookup() (the \"default\", no-argument, opt-out entry points) must NOT resolve \"script\", \"dns\", or \"url\" lookup prefixes against attacker-influenced template text unless the caller has explicitly opted in (e.g. via interpolatorStringLookup(Set<DefaultStringLookup>) or an explicit allow-list/EnumSet parameter naming those lookups). Concretely: after the fix, `StringSubstitutor.createInterpolator().replace(\"${script:javascript:...}\")` should leave the token unresolved/return it unchanged (or throw a well-defined exception) rather than invoking ScriptEngine.eval(); likewise for `${dns:...}` and `${url:...}`. Known bypass variants the fix must also block / that a patch review should re-check: (1) case/whitespace variants of the prefix \u2014 InterpolatorStringLookup.toKey() lower-cases keys, so \"Script:\", \"SCRIPT:\", \" script :\" must still be blocked, not just exact-case \"script\"; (2) the split-on-first-colon parsing in ScriptStringLookup.lookup() (key.split(SPLIT_STR, 2)) \u2014 engine names other than \"javascript\"/\"JavaScript\" (e.g. \"nashorn\", \"js\", \"graal.js\" if present on the classpath) must be equally blocked, since the guard should be at the lookup-registration/dispatch level, not by engine name; (3) nested/recursive interpolation \u2014 a value pulled from another enabled lookup (e.g. \"sys\", \"env\", \"properties\") that itself contains \"${script:...}\" and gets re-substituted must not re-enable script execution; (4) programmatic bypass \u2014 code that calls StringLookupFactory.INSTANCE.scriptStringLookup() or the package-private ScriptStringLookup.INSTANCE directly, or builds a custom StringLookup map still containing \"script\"/\"dns\"/\"url\" via addDefaultStringLookups(), must be covered by documentation/deprecation or a runtime guard, since it circumvents an interpolator-only fix; (